<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="9940dff1">Sufficient information may have been collected when the project was initially planned and estimated. Leverage this
information before asking the client to provide it again. Also review relevant project management documents including the
charter and plan if available.</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="898845cd"></attribute>
<attribute name="usageGuidance" nodeId="aeb2cac6"></attribute>
<attribute name="refinedDescription" nodeId="3400a417">&lt;p&gt;
    Begin by listing out the key pieces of information or data needed along with the potential source(s) of this
    information (if known) and then:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Determine if the desired information already exists and if so, is the most current information readily available?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Does the existing information need to be supplemented to establish a more complete picture of the current state and
        better understand the needs of the organization? If so, consider conducting interviews or workshops with key
        stakeholders.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Does the information need to be collected on a larger scale? If so, surveys could be administered to obtain a more
        holistic view.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is the information collected frequently enough?
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Is the information maintained manually or through systems that are updated regularly?
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
</Element>
